CONVERSATION:
[10:35] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: morning
[10:59] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: anybody knows if there is some macro/function to swap the byteorder?
[11:00] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: void QDataStream::setByteOrder ( ByteOrder bo )
[11:02] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: after that writing to that stream should use the set byte order
[11:02] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: that seems a bit overkill ;)
[11:04] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: why?
[11:04] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: you can check the byte order from QSysInfo
[11:04] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: I just need to swap one variable in a loop
[11:04] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: htonl, htons, ntohl, ntohs - convert values between host and network byte order
[11:06] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: doesn't do much on a ppc mac, I'm afraid
[11:06] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: why not?
[11:07] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: isn't it posix?
[11:07] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: it is posix. but network and host byte order is the same on a ppc, iirc
[11:07] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: well, and?
[11:08] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: so if I change from NETWORK to HOST byte order, and both are the same.. ;)
[11:08] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: isn't the whole idea to have your code portable so that when you use it on some other system one day the ordering is still the same
[11:08] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: no, the idea is to get mp3 playback working
[11:08] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: yes, but adding in thos NOP:s will make it more portable
[11:09] e5c246c422bf5aeed2dab4c68c680e2b: if the idea is just to do an always-swap-order-and-not-convert-to-a.-common-format, then just do some bit wanking
[11:09] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: no, the idea is to swap the byte order on ppc, and don't swap it on x86
[11:10] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: I'm not sure what you mean by "always swap order and not convert to a common format"
[11:10] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db6144125e4: I'm not sure what you mean by "always swap order and not convert to a common format"
[11:10] 6e39fc182cde48f018c30db")
