[12:51] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: hi. looking at html 5 and playing around a bit. i noticed that html5 &quot;valid global date and time strings&quot; are not understood by the js date object. is there a reason for this? i was trying to make nice dates like everyone else, &quot;2 hours ago&quot; etc, using the datetime attribute value. maybe i'm abusing it? :)
[13:15] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: datetime on which element?
[13:17] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the datetime attribute on &lt;time&gt;
[13:18] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: with values like &quot;2006-09-24T05:00-07:00&quot;
[13:19] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you'll be able to get a Date object off it using t.date+t.time, once it's implemented
[13:19] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: though maybe we should offer a simple way of getting the exact corresponding Date
[13:23] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: i see. i understand most of it is not implemented yet, but with the focus on backward compatibility i wasn't sure if it was on purpose or not to use a format Date doesn't understand :)
[13:24] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's the format HTML4 used for datetime=&quot;&quot; on &lt;ins&gt; and &lt;del&gt;
[13:24] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: (ISO8601)
[13:24] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: oh.. i didn't even know about that
[13:25] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: i wouldn't be surprised if js didn't change to support it at some point, it's a pretty standard format
[13:25] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: but anyway, there'll be DOM APIs to give you a Date in due course
[13:25] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: they're just not implemented yet
[13:26] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: bed time for me now
[13:26] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: nn
[13:26] a5218d2a72481c137ed6383d3526a093: gn, thanks for the help!

