CONVERSATION:
[23:38] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e, since you're there. I was wondering if you know the background behind &quot;The handling of pixel rounding when the specified coordinates do not exactly map to the device coordinate space is not defined by this specification&quot; at http://www.whatwg.org/specs/web-apps/current-work/#dom-context-2d-putimagedata I submitted a bug report regarding it since it causes odd behavior between browsers like FF that use bilinear filtering.
[23:39] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: yeah it's so that if you're on a highres display, the browser can use whatever algorithm is most optimal for hte platform when deciding how to round CSS pixels to device pixel boundaries
[23:39] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: I can whip up an example that produces artifacts in browsers because of how they interpret dx and dy in drawImage and other functions
[23:39] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the paragraph above only refers to the putImageData/getImageData stuff
[23:39] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: doesn'ta ffect drawImage
[23:40] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: yeah this is probably a complaint that should be made with browsers maybe :\
[23:40] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: yeah but drawImage follows the same undefined rules
[23:44] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it does?
[23:44] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it shouldn't
[23:44] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: where does it say that it does?
[23:44] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: checks
[23:48] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: http://www.assaultwars.com/javascriptgame/tests/tiling.html &lt;-- example though. Rendering offset in FF using drawImage uses bilinear filtering unlike chrome and other browsers which use nearest neighbor :\
[23:49] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: Not sure if that's intended to allow browsers to be flexible in what they do or not
[23:50] d4b206c408eda02de41d1a5a77624aac: Actually chrome is the only modern browser that uses nearest neighbor. It's as if it Math.round's the dx and dy
[23:51] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: that's basically antialiasing
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: we don't currently specify how that should work
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: similar issue as the putImageData() stuff, but not quite the same
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's not a bug, it's a feature
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's a feature
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: it's a feature
[23:52] 69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52")
