conv_id=1321 | raw="
CONVERSATION:
[01:46] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: Why does lxml's XMLParser seem to think the document consists solely of the &lt;head&gt; element?
[01:47] a924fb58d2c25874322f4e5126f608b9: Is there a &lt;html&gt; element?
[01:47] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: Yes
[01:47] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: There's a &lt;body&gt; element too
[01:47] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: but doc.find('body') returns None
[01:48] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: gives up trying to fix the spec-splitter properly, and just uses lxml's HTMLParser with recover=True and hopes it doesn't misparse anything seriously
[16:55] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: Hixie: http://forum.doom9.org/showthread.php?t=122363 seems a useful explanation - there is a secret fixed set of possible processing keys, and each can be 'reached' (in some mathematical way I don't quite understand) from a different subset of all device keys; so you can revoke compromised devices by choosing a (small) set of processing keys such that none can be reached from any of the device keys known to any compromised device.
[16:55] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: Once you know a processing key that's used on a disc (which is typically the same for all discs), you can combine that with some other disc-specific data to end up with the keys that decrypt the actual data; at least until new discs are manufactured with new processing keys, in which case you have to start all over again and try to discover the new keys.
[16:56] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: (...or at least that's my understanding - it looks like a not entirely trivial system)
[17:04] a924fb58d2c25874322f4e5126f608b9: Security through really many keys
[17:21] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: At least it's a readable published specification, and the security depends entirely on the keys being kept secret, which is how cryptography ought to work - the problem is that it's impossible to keep the keys secret, and it has statements like &quot;A device shall keep the value s_0 confidential, as defined in the license agreement&quot; which doesn't work quite so well when millions of customers have your devices and want to extract the secret values
[17:22] a924fb58d2c25874322f4e5126f608b9: As the aacs people say, the system hasn't been breached, it's key leakage. They just leave out the 'inevitable'.
[17:22] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: So apart from being fundamentally flawed, it seems quite good :-)
[15:35] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: HTML5 parsing is incompatible with IE and Opera on http://www.gerv.net/security/content-restrictions/ - it doesn't think half the page is blue
[15:39] a924fb58d2c25874322f4e5126f608b9: ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: I think it's a bug in the parser, not the spec
[15:40] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: I think it's a bug in the parser, not the spec
[15:40] ab7faae5d7b250ea8606486575f8f79c: I think it's a bug in the parser, not the spec
")
