[01:09] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: hello
[01:09] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: i'm implementing a browser automation tool that has a quasi-DOM as part of its API
[01:09] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: my idea is to generate that code from the XHTML and/or HTML5 spec, keeping it easy to maintain
[01:10] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: at the minimum i'll just need a map of { ¬´tag name¬ª: [¬´attribute¬ª, ...], ... }, but also some type info would be nice (e.g. 'disabled' =&gt; boolean)
[01:10] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: any tips on where to start or any code that does something similar already would be greatly appreciated
[01:11] ef6f2287bae00ba79475fbf56c2b6fee: ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: what happens if you need to automate something involving elements/attributes the browser supports that aren't conforming in HTML5?
[01:12] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: ef6f2287bae00ba79475fbf56c2b6fee: probably add it manually or have the user do browser.attributeValue(&quot;some-attribute&quot;)
[01:12] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: s/browser/element
[01:13] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: at the moment i'm just playing with the idea.. having at least some part of the code generated from the spec
[01:14] ae8e7b83a7cd383aca11b86b2a32395d: am i heading down the wrong road here?
