CONVERSATION:
[13:01] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: are there actual implementations that support SVG 1.2 Tiny scripting via Java jars?
[13:02] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: If I had to guess, I'd guess that scripting via ECMAScript would be a better match for Gecko/WebKit/Opera
[13:03] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9, it doesn't do 1.2 Tiny (just 1.1 full plus a couple of 1.2-isms), but Batik does support the scripting via Java jars
[13:05] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: OK. interesting.
[13:05] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: i don't know if many people use the features though
[13:05] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: how do they handle security? that seems like a fast way to classloader hell
[13:05] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: s/features/feature/
[13:06] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: i believe it has the same restrictions as that of ecmascript running in the document
[13:08] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: i.e. those classes won't be able to write to the filesystem, open non-same-origin connections, etc.
[10:13] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: &quot;SVG would probably have remained a dead technology if Google hadn't started carrying its banner with Chrome&quot;
[10:13] b8810fee2f4a71f849f3f7409546d1d9: eh?
[10:15] a3f19f90edf0a0893c7847d08016abe3: s/Google/MS/ and s/Chrome/IE/? :)
